            We will now focus on particular kinds of $h$-formulas, those only involving logical connectives and equality. As stated in the introduction, it turns out that whether or not a certain class of structures recognizes coordinates is equivalent to whether or not a particular formula is logically equivalent to an $h$-formula. To be more formal, we must first defined a theory relative to an indexed family of structures and a fixed ideal.            

	\begin{definition}\label{Def:ThI}
        Let $\Index{I}$ be an indexed set, $\Ideal{I}$ be an ideal on $\Index{I}$, and $(\mathcal{M}_i)_{i \in \Index{I}}$ be an indexed family of $\mathcal{L}$-structures. We let $\Th_\Ideal{I}(\mathcal{M}_i, i\in \mathbb{I})$ denote the common theory of $\mathcal{M}_i$ for all but $\Ideal{I}$-many $i\in \Index{I}$.  In other words,
		\[
		\Th_\Ideal{I}(\mathcal{M}_i, i \in \mathbb{I}):=\{\phi\mid  \{i \in \Index{I} \mid \mathcal{M}_i \models \neg \phi\} \in \Ideal{I}\}.
		\] 
        We sometimes write $\Th_{\Ideal{I}}(\mathcal{M}_i, i \in \Index{I})$ as $\Th_{\Ideal{I}}(\mathcal{M}_i)$ when there is no possibility for confusion.  
	\end{definition}

        In practice, there are two variants of $h$-formulas in the language of equality which will become quite useful. These $h$-formulas should be thought of as \emph{comparing elements in the reduced product on coordinates}. The next proposition shows that if the \emph{relative comparison formula} is an $h$-formula then the \emph{comparison formula} is an $h$-formula. 
        \begin{proposition}\label{prop:interpretingAllZsupport} 
		Let $\Index{I}$ be an indexed set, $\Ideal{I}$ be an ideal on $\Index{I}$, and $(\mathcal{M}_i)_{i \in \Index{I}}$ be an indexed family of $\mathcal{L}$-structures. Suppose that for all but $\Ideal{I}$-many $i\in \Index{I}$, $\mathcal{M}_i$ has at least $3$ elements and that the formula $x=z \rightarrow y=z$ is equivalent to an $h$-formula modulo $\Th_\Ideal{I}(\mathcal{M}_i, i\in \mathbb{I})$. Then the formula  $x=z \rightarrow y=w$ is  equivalent to an $h$-formula modulo $\Th_\Ideal{I}(\mathcal{M}_i, i\in \mathbb{I})$. 
			\end{proposition}

	\begin{proof} 
		The proposition is the immediate consequence of the following two claims. 

		\begin{claim}
			With respect to the theory of any structure $\mathcal{M}$ with at least two elements, the formula $x \neq y$ is equivalent to $$\psi_{\neq}(x,y):= (\forall z) \ (x=y \rightarrow z=y),$$ and is therefore equivalent to an $h$-formula.
		\end{claim}
            \begin{proof}
                By assumption $(x=y \rightarrow z=y)$ is equivalent to an $h$-formula $\phi(x,y,z)$. Therefore, from the definition of $h$-formulas, $(\forall z) \phi(x,y,z)$ is an $h$-formula equivalent to $\psi_{\neq}(x,y)$. Since $\mathcal{M}$ has at least two elements, the latter is clearly equivalent to $x\neq y$.
            \end{proof}



				\begin{claim}
					 With respect to the theory of any structure $\mathcal{M}$ with at least three elements, the formula $x=z \rightarrow y=w$ is equivalent to the following formula:
					\begin{align*}
						(\exists u)(\exists x')(\exists y') & ~\quad \psi_{\neq}(u,x)\wedge \psi_{\neq}(u,y)
						\\
						&\wedge   (x=z \leftrightarrow x'=z) \wedge  (\forall x'') \left ( (x=z \leftrightarrow x''=z) \rightarrow (x''=u \rightarrow x'=u) \right)\\
						\tag{*} \label{eq.Zsupp}&\wedge   (y=w \leftrightarrow y'=w) \wedge (\forall y'') \left ( (y=w \leftrightarrow y''=w) \rightarrow (y''=u \rightarrow y'=u)\right)\\
						& \wedge  y'=u \rightarrow x'=u.
					\end{align*}
										%   It follows that $x=z \rightarrow y=w$ is equivalent to an $\Ideal{h}$-formula.
