starting by stipulating that it is infinite in the case that $z_{\alpha}: \mathbb{R}^+ \to \mathbb{R}^+$ is not absolutely continuous for any $\alpha\in\mathscr{R}$. %Next, we stipulate that $\mathcal{J}(z,x,u) = \infty$ in the case that $(z,x,u) \notin \bar{\Upsilon}$, where $\bar{\Upsilon}$ is the closure of $\hat{\Upsilon} \subset \Upsilon$, i.e.
