\section{Introduction}
%=============================================================================================================================
 Recent advancements in laser technology facilitate the study of the interaction between high-power, ultrahigh contrast femtosecond pulses with solid-density matter \cite{drake2006introduction, kaw2017nonlinear, ravindra2009intense,rocca2024ultra}. This opens up avenues in high-energy density science, with potential applications in high-brightness vacuum ultraviolet (VUV) and X-ray sources \cite{dromey2007bright}, attosecond optics \cite{thaury2007plasma}, tabletop ultrafast X-ray \cite{kneip2010bright,kneip2008observation} imaging and ultrafast diffraction \cite{gaffney2007imaging}. These developments are critically dependent on the temporal and spatial characteristics of femtosecond pulses \cite{jolly2020spatio}. Unlike picosecond and nanosecond pulses, where interaction occurs in plasma that expands below solid density, ultrahigh contrast femtosecond pulses induce nearly instantaneous excitation of solid-density matter \cite{murnane1991ultrafast}. In addition, short-pulse, high-intensity lasers can create conditions where quantum electrodynamics (QED) effects \cite{marklund2009quantum} and relativistic effects \cite{umstadter2001review} become significant. The extreme pressure (of the order of several 100 Mbar) exerted on the plasma surface leads to surface deformation resulting in various instabilities\cite{tatarakis2003propagation}. Once the plasma is formed, the laser-plasma interaction is predominantly governed by the critical density surface, which acts as a reflective and absorptive interface—referred to as the `plasma mirror' (PM). The dynamic relativistic PM in turn severely modifies the spectral and temporal properties of the laser pulse \cite{thaury2007plasma}. Therefore, it becomes important to accurately measure the spatial, spectral and temporal characteristics of these relativistic PMs. At these relativistic intensities, the ponderomotive force (PMF), a nonlinear force arising from the interaction of the laser field and charged particles, plays a crucial role in governing the physical phenomena such as nonlinear self-action effects, particle acceleration, multiphoton ionization and chaotic laser plasma dynamics.

 Phenomena such as radiation pressure acceleration (RPA) \cite{esirkepov2004highly,robinson2008radiation,macchi2013ion}, high harmonic generation (HHG)\cite{thaury2007plasma}, and laser absorption mechanisms like resonance absorption \cite{gibbon1992collisionless} are highly affected by the curvature introduced by the PMF. Significant progress has been made in probing these ultrafast dynamics. Techniques based on spectral measurements \cite{liu1992competition,malka1996experimental,adak2015terahertz,dulat2022subpicosecond,mondal2010doppler} and HHG-based diagnostics \cite{vincenti2014optical,chopineau2021spatio} have been explored. However, spectrum-based methods are inherently one-dimensional, providing only velocity information, while HHG-based techniques offer two-dimensional insights but fail to directly measure the curved geometry of plasma mirrors. Due to these challenges and the lack of direct measurements, the community often relies on simplified theoretical models and simulations \cite{wilks1992absorption,macchi2005laser,quesnel1998theory,fedorov1997ponderomotive,schumacher2011shaped,kingham2001phase}. These approaches, however, are contingent upon various assumptions and initial conditions, which may not fully capture the complexities of the interaction. 


