In this work, we report the first direct, \textit{in situ} three-dimensional surface mapping of a plasma mirror during femtosecond pulse interactions by measuring the wavefront of the reflected light from the plasma. The wavefront carries the information of surface depth and provide a direct and easy measure of the plasma surface. The deformation has been measured for different intensities and has been shown to match the theoretical model developed in \cite{vincenti2014optical}.
We also measured the spectrum and temporal profile of the reflected pulse to gain deeper insight into the underlying plasma dynamics. The reflected pulse undergoes significant modifications in both the spectral and temporal domains. For comparison, an ultrahigh-contrast second harmonic pulse was employed to examine the spatial and spectral properties of the reflected beam. In addition our high-fidelity 3D3V and 2D3V particle-in-cell (PIC) simulations successfully model the laser-induced depression of plasma density surfaces caused by the PMF. Additionally spectral analysis of reflected field indicate the presence of odd higher harmonics.
