\section{Experimental Details}
%=============================================================================================================================

The experiments were performed with the multi-terawatt laser system at Tata Institute of Fundamental Research, Mumbai\cite{chatterjee2014high}. A Ti:Sapphire laser (30 fs , 800 nm) with high picosecond contrast ($10^{-9}$) and producing relativistic intensities, was used to create overdense plasma on 5 mm thick solid glass (BK7) targets. The laser was focused with an f/3 off-axis dielectric-coated parabola to a 7 $\mu m$ spot (FWHM) (Figure~\ref{fig1}b) to achieve different intensities. The laser reflected from the target plasma was collected and sent outside the vacuum chamber. The beam was imaged on a wavefront measuring device phasics SID-4 (Figure~\ref{fig1}a). 
%newly added
The beam was also sent to Ocean Optics HR 2000 spectrometer and a single shot temporal profile measuring device GRENOUILLE \cite{trebino2000frequency}.


We use a high definition wavefront measuring device also known as QuadriWave Lateral Shearing Interferometer (QWLSI), invented by Primot et al. in 2000\cite{primot2000extended}\cite{primot1995achromatic}. The QWLSI is composed of a two dimensional transmission grating, a phase checkerboard, and a CCD device. Four replicas of the sample wavefront to be measured are produced and the interference pattern is recorded on a CCD camera. The period of the phase checkerboard is adjusted such that 4 waves are generated from 4 first-order diffraction orders of the incoming wave (see Figure~\ref{fig1}d). The wavefront is retrieved from the interferogram by properly masking the spatial frequencies in the Fourier domain and integrating the gradients along the transverse axis.  The combination of the grating and phase checkerboard is called a modified Hartmann mask (MHM) due to its similarity with the traditional Hartmann sensors. As QWLSI wavefront devices are very sensitive (5 nm surface RMS) and have a high spatial resolution (27 $\mu m$), these devices are routinely used in adaptive optics setups for wavefront correction in high-intensity lasers where the wavefront becomes heavily distorted by the different amplification stages \cite{yoon2021realization}. They also are used extensively in biology for phase microscopy\cite{baffou2023wavefront}.

