As seen in Figure~\ref{res_fig1}a, the wavefront becomes increasingly curved, indicating that the plasma surface is pushed towards the target as the intensity increases \textcolor{black}{(note: for better visualization, the plot has been flipped upside down along the Z-axis)}. The lineouts of the plots are shown in Figure~\ref{res_fig1}c. Due to the PMF, density steepening occurs, which results in the curvature of the plasma surface. After the pulse propagates, the hot plasma begins to expand exponentially toward the vacuum, establishing a density gradient. 


A part of the reflected light is also sent to a spectrometer to analyze the spectral behavior of the plasma mirror, as shown in Figure~\ref{spectrum_fig}. The laser spectrum is significantly modified by the plasma surface. At high intensity, the spectrum broadens, and one part of it (around 825 nm) undergoes heavy absorption. As the intensity decreases, the spectrum starts to resemble the original laser spectrum. The spectral broadening can be explained by self-phase modulation in the plasma leading to possibilities of pulse compression with appropriate chirp compensation. 
To measure the temporal profile of the reflected laser after the interaction, the collected light was collimated and was sent to a GRENOUILLE (Frequency Resolved Optical Gating) device. %think to put
Because of the collection optics and chamber window the pulse gets stretched before reaching the detector, but still gives insight about the pulse profile at different intensities.

