Phototrophic biological cells act as dissipative structures. Energy coming in as sunlight is captured as high potential electrons that can then be used to generate ATP via generation of a protonmotive force. For many phototrophs, this energy is dissipated in part by reducing CO$_2$ to biomass in an ATP-requiring manner. During this process, the CBB cycle acts as a dissipative cycle. Dissipative cycles occur throughout the cell, from the CBB and Arnon-Buchanan cycles of CO$_2$ assimilation \cite{Bar-Even_2012}, to the oxidative TCA cycle and many others (\textit{e.g.,} see Bar-Even \textit{et al.} \cite{Bar-Even_2012}). Whether flux through the TCA cycle is thermodynamically favored in the oxidative (clockwise) direction to oxidize metabolites to CO$_2$ and produce NAD(P)H or in the reductive (counterclockwise, Arnon-Buchanan cycle) direction to assimilate CO$_2$ and utilize NAD(P)H depends on the concentration of redox carriers in the system (i.e. NAD(P)+:NAD(P)H ratio).
%The oxidative TCA cycle acts unidirectionally (i.e., not as a cycle) until enough of the most oxidized product, succinate, builds up to a level that makes the production of fumarate possible, which then builds until malate synthesis and oxaloactetate synthesis are possible, at which point the cycle becomes active and dissipates energy in a rapid cyclic and autocatalytic nature. In these systems, the flux is an emergent property of the thermodynamics. A thermodynamically driven model ensures that the model works for the right physical reasons. Because entropy is simply a measure of probability, a maximum entropy model is simply the most probable model that can be observed. When implemented for metabolism, a maximum entropy model provides the most likely set of rate constants, much like ensemble modeling but without the large search over parameter space. When combined with Metabolic Control Analysis and optimization approaches such as optimal control or reinforcement learning, the thermodynamics leads predictions of how a cell must be regulated in order to operate \cite{Britton2020, King_frontiersSysBio_2023}.  

During growth on malate, isotope labeling studies have been used with models to infer the direction of flux in the central metabolism of \textit{R. rubrum} \cite{McCulley2020}. The models indicated that the isotope pattern was most similar to a model in which malate entered into carbon metabolism via the TCA cycle as expected, but then flowed through reactions in opposite redox directions, both oxidatively to oxaloacetate and reductively to succinate.  
If sufficient reducing equivalents were present, then one would expect malate to proceed primarily reductively through the Arnon-Buchanan cycle. Thus, understanding and predicting the dynamics of redox carrier concentration (i.e. redox balance) and carbon flow is a challenge for PNSB, and has remained largely an open question. 

%How \textit{R. rubrum} provides sufficient electrons for such reduction processes is an open question. \textit{R. rubrum} cannot provide limitless electrons through phototrophic water splitting. Alternately, \textit{R. rubrum} must either extract high energy electrons from H$_2$ via hydrogenase, pull electrons from the Q pool generated by photochemical excitation of elections derived from lower-energy donors like sulfide or thiosulfate (reverse electron flow) \cite{hansen1972}, or must extract electrons from the oxidation of organic carbon substrates \cite{wang1993}.% 

A similar issue of how redox balance is maintained and which direction of carbon flows exists for photoheterotrophic growth of \textit{R. rubrum} on acetate and ethanol. Acetate and ethanol are assimilated as acetyl-CoA and then the ethylmalonyl-CoA pathway condenses 2 molecules of acetyl-CoA followed by reduction to malate and succinate, which enter the TCA cycle and are used to form biomass.  Again, provided that sufficient reducing equivalents are available, one might intuitively expect that a significant percentage of the malate and succinate would proceed through the TCA cycle in the reductive direction (Arnon-Buchanan cycle).

