As discussed in \S\ref{subsec:mcparch}, the MCP architecture consists of three components: the Host (running the AI application), the Client (which communicates with the Server), and the Server (the backend plugin handler). Users interact with the AI through the Host, which parses inputs and delegates tool invocations to the Server via the Client. Since MCP Servers often run locally with high privileges, they inherently trust third-party code—creating opportunities for abuse by malicious or poorly implemented plugins. To mitigate the growing security risks in the MCP ecosystem, we initiate a study from a foundational perspective: analyzing the system-level API behaviors of MCP applications. Unlike mobile platforms such as Android, which enforce runtime permission checks and app-level isolation, MCP applications (particularly the Server components) often run natively on users' local machines with full access privileges. This architecture assumes implicit trust, but lacks any built-in mechanism to constrain what third-party plugins can access or perform. As a result, even a seemingly benign plugin can invoke sensitive APIs to read arbitrary files, initiate outbound network connections, monitor memory activity, or interact with system-level processes, without user awareness or consent.

These risks are not hypothetical. For example, a plugin claiming to “help search local documents” could scan the entire file system, exfiltrate browser history or SSH keys, or manipulate system configuration files. A plugin designed to “automate Git workflows” could silently install SSH backdoors, modify repositories, or initiate unauthorized pushes. More subtly, a ``\textit{code generation}'' plugin could embed telemetry beacons or leak intermediate data to remote servers. In the absence of sandboxing, any such behaviors are entirely at the discretion of the plugin developer—posing a serious threat to both individual users and enterprise environments.
What makes this threat surface especially concerning is its stealth and accessibility. The open nature of MCP encourages rapid tool development, but security vetting is minimal, and the protocol offers no standardized way to declare or restrict system API usage. From a security standpoint, this creates a blind spot at the edge of the AI-agent interaction boundary—where high-level natural language commands are translated into low-level system actions by untrusted code.


