\section{Discussion}

\textbf{Software Engineering Insights}

Through large-scale static code analysis, this study systematically identified the threat and risk distribution in the MCP Server ecosystem, offering several lessons:

First, system and network resource API risks are most prominent, covering the majority of MCP Servers. Developers heavily rely on low-level system commands and port communication, but permission isolation and security checks are generally weak. Therefore, \textbf{least-privilege API usage, security auditing, and access control must be prioritized} in MCP Server/plugin development and operation.

Second, API call patterns differ significantly by industry and function. Developer tools, API development, and productivity/workflow apps are most active, especially with file/system resource risks, while user-facing domains (e-commerce, social media management) have higher network communication risks. Security measures should be tailored to the business scenario.

Third, analysis of user preference (GitHub Stars) and dangerous API presence shows that popularity does not guarantee security—some highly starred MCP Servers still use many high-risk APIs. The open-source community and users should not rely solely on popularity as a security reference but should strengthen code audits and monitoring.

Finally, practical attack scenarios confirm that high-risk API calls can cause repository tampering, malicious content delivery, and phishing, validating static analysis as an effective risk identification tool and highlighting the urgent need for least-privilege and API access controls.

\textbf{Ethical Considerations}

This research strictly follows responsible security research ethics: all experiments were conducted on self-owned or authorized public MCP Server/plugin code, without affecting third-party production environments, user data, or commercial services. High-risk findings were not exploited, and specific attack details are not disclosed. All risks involving commercial or open-source projects follow responsible disclosure procedures.


