\begin{abstract}
% In recent years, the integration of analytical control theory with learning-based control methods has gradually emerged as a highly promising strategy for addressing such control problems. 
% The idea of integrating analytical control theory with learning-based control is becoming a promising method for control of partial differential equation (PDE) systems with infinite dimension. 
This paper develops a reinforcement learning-based controller for the stabilization of partial differential equation (PDE) systems. Within the soft actor-critic (SAC) framework, we embed a DeepONet, a well-known neural operator (NO), which is pretrained using the backstepping controller. The pretrained DeepONet captures the essential features of the backstepping controller and serves as a feature extractor, replacing the convolutional neural networks (CNNs) layers in the original actor and critic networks, and directly connects to the fully connected layers of the SAC architecture. We apply this novel backstepping and reinforcement learning integrated method to stabilize an unstable first-order hyperbolic PDE and an unstable reaction-diffusion PDE. Simulation results demonstrate that the proposed method outperforms the standard SAC, SAC with an untrained DeepONet, and the backstepping controller on both systems.  


% The idea of integrating analytical control theory with learning-based control is becoming a promising method for control of PDEs with infinite dimension. However, how to  In this paper, we design a integrated control strategy combining PDE backstepping  and deep reinforcement learning (RL) for an unstable first-order hyperbolic PDE with spatially-varying delays.  This method eliminates extra constraint on the delay function required for the backstepping design. We embed a DeepONet, trained to learn the backstepping controller, into a soft actor-critic (SAC) framework as a feature extractor for both the actor and critic networks. Simulation results demonstrate that the proposed algorithm outperforms standard SAC in reducing steady-state error and surpasses the backstepping controller in mitigating overshoot.
%  This soft actor-critic with backstepping-pretrained DeepONet for control of Partial Differential Equations

% validate the effectiveness of a control approach based on neural operators by combining PDE backstepping control strategies with reinforcement learning for a class of unstable first-order hyperbolic PDEs and parabolic partial differential equations (PDEs). To overcome the computational complexity and model dependency associated with backstepping control when dealing with these PDEs, we introduce a neural operator network (DeepONet) to approximate the backstepping controller. DeepONet is capable of extracting key features from the backstepping controller and efficiently passing these features to the policy network. In this way, we construct a reinforcement learning framework that can adapt to dynamic system changes in real time.In the experimental validation, we designed detailed simulation scenarios for the first-order hyperbolic PDE and the parabolic PDE, respectively. The results show that the reinforcement learning method based on neural operators outperforms both the Soft Actor-Critic (SAC) algorithm augmented with DeepONet but without pretraining, and the baseline SAC algorithm in both experiments.
