      \href@noop {} {}\bibinfo {note} {{See Supplemental Material for additional information. In Sec.~I, we provide a detailed diagrammatic analysis of the electron self-energy. In Secs.~II--VI, we derive the isotropic adiabatic and vertex-corrected Eliashberg equations, including their simplifications and Matsubara summation procedures. Sections~VII-IX present convergence tests for harmonic and anharmonic phonons, analysis of the Eliashberg spectral function, and superconducting properties with vertex corrections for H$_3$S at 200~GPa. Figure~S1 illustrates the Feynman diagrams contributing to the electron self-energy, along with schematic representations of the associated Fermi-surface scattering processes. Figure~S2 shows the sign convention for the Levi-Civita symbol $\epsilon_{ijk}$ based on cyclic permutations. Figure~S3 presents the convergence of the harmonic phonon spectra of H$_3$S with respect to the $\mathbf{q}$-point grid and the kinetic energy cutoff. Figure~S4 justifies the use of a coarser
      $\mathbf{k}$-mesh ($12 \times 12 \times 12$) for anharmonic calculations in H$_3$S and examines the effect of varying the displacement amplitude from 0.01~\AA{} to 0.2~\AA. Figure~S5 shows the convergence of the anharmonic phonon spectra of H$_3$S with respect to supercell size and iterative self-consistency, achieved using a linear mixing scheme. Figure~S6 compares the Eliashberg spectral function in H$_3$S computed using different approaches. Figure~S7 presents the superconducting gap in H$_3$S computed using the FSR, FBW, and FBW+$\mu$ approaches with harmonic and anharmonic phonons, both with and without vertex corrections.}}\BibitemShut {Stop}%
