\subsection{Binary Simulations}
We used the binary stellar population synthesis code {\tt POSYDON v1.0} \citep{Fragos_2023} to search for possible binary stellar systems that can reproduce W2's observed SED\null. {\tt POSYDON} employs the stellar-structure code Modules for Experiments in Stellar Astrophysics \citep[MESA;][]{Paxton2011,Paxton2013,Paxton_2015,Paxton2018,Paxton2019,Jermyn2023} to create grids of binary stellar-evolution models with separate grids created for distinct phases of binary evolution. The simulated stars generated by {\tt POSYDON v1.0} have stellar metallicity fixed to the solar value, while the nebular oxygen abundance in the Warhol arc is slightly subsolar, $\log(Z_{\rm neb}/Z_\odot)=-0.24\pm0.08$ (\Pa). We do not expect this small difference to significantly affect the results.

We used  {\tt POSYDON} to generate a synthetic population of 100,000 binary stellar systems. The initial mass of Star~1 ($M1$) was stochastically drawn from the \cite{Kroupa_2001} initial mass function (IMF), initial mass ratios $q\equiv M2/M1$ were drawn from a flat distribution spanning the range 0.1--1.0, and initial orbital periods ($P$) were drawn from the log-space power law described by \cite{Sana_2013}. The minimum period was set such that zero-age main-sequence systems did not undergo Roche-lobe overflow, and the maximum period was set to 6000 days. For each initial binary configuration, {\tt POSYDON} used nearest-neighbor interpolation to the closest MESA binary grid, tracking its (downsampled) evolution from the zero-age main-sequence (ZAMS) state to the final state as a disrupted binary, binary  merger, or until the formation of a compact object (white dwarf, neutron star, or black hole). 

To match W2, we searched the 100,000 simulated binaries   for systems where the temperatures of the two stars at any epoch of evolution were within 2$\sigma$ of W2's stellar temperatures (\Pa). 
%($\log(T_{\rm eff})=3.55^{+0.06}_{-0.08}$ and $\log(T_{\rm eff})=4.10^{+0.23}_{-0.13}$), 
The simulated temperature, luminosity, mass, and radius of each star in the selected systems were used to 
generate a model stellar spectrum using {\tt pystellibs} to interpolate the BaSeL stellar library \citep{Lejeune_1998}. After applying {\tt pysynphot}\footnote{https://github.com/spacetelescope/pysynphot} to generate each star's synthetic NIRCam photometry in each filter from the spectra,
