The optimal terminal wealth $X^{*,\lambda}(T)$ for primal problem (\ref{util}) is given by Proposition (\ref{dong}), that is, 
	$X^{*, \lambda}(T)  = x^{*, \lambda}(Y(T))$ and $y_0$ is determined by the binding budget constraint $\mathbb{E}[X^{*,\lambda}(T) Y(T)]=x_0y_0$. The optimal wealth process $X^{*,\lambda}$ can be determined by $X^{*,\lambda}(t)=\mathbb{E}[X^{*,\lambda}(T)Y(T)/Y(t)|{{\cal{F}}_t^S}]$ for $t\in[0,T]$ and the optimal control process $\pi^{*,\lambda}$ is determined by the martingale representation theorem.

