and $W = (W_1, W_2)^\top$  a two-dimensional standard Brownian motion under the physical measure $\mathbb{P}$. We assume that the $(\sigma_1(\mu_1))^2$ and $(\sigma_2(\mu_2))^2$ are linear in $\mu_1$ and $\mu_2$ respectively.  It follows that the covariance matrix $a(\mu) = \sigma(\mu)\sigma(\mu)^\top$ has an affine dependence on $\mu$, i.e., 
	\begin{align*}
	a(\mu) =  A^0 + A^1\mu_1 + A^2 \mu_2
	\end{align*}
	for some two-dimensional constant matrices $A^0$, $A^1$, and $A^2 \in \mathbb{R}^{2\times 2}$. For any row vector $u \in \mathbb{R}^2$, we define the row vector
	\[ A(u) = (uA^1u^\top, uA^2u^\top).\]
